I reviewed 00012 and the actual PR #3 code, not just Claude’s summary. The implementation is a strong first store-engine pass: the PostgreSQL transaction boundary is clean, address caches are SET-based, txid/wtxid separation is preserved, P2QPK witness storage remains byte-exact, and the reorg path keeps immutable orphan history. Claude also reports all A–X tests and the uncached quality gates passing.

GitHub currently shows PR #3 open, unmerged, non-draft, and mergeable at the expected single head commit 0878d4516fdaa3c3acce8e3972bba6a5633061ac, based directly on the merged Phase 2B.1 baseline.

I would not merge yet. I found four store-level correctness gaps that the current tests don't exercise.

ApplyBlock can corrupt canonical state by applying an old orphaned block below the current tip. It only reads the current checkpoint height; it does not require the incoming block to extend the current tip, and it only writes the checkpoint when block.Height >= currentCheckpointHeight. That creates a dangerous case: apply A1, roll it back, apply replacement B1/B2, then call ApplyBlock(A1) again. A1 is now orphaned and below the B2 checkpoint. applyBlockHeader sees the already-existing header and accepts it without checking/promoting canonical; its outputs can be recreated in utxo_state, address caches recomputed, and because its height is below the checkpoint the final checkpoint validation is skipped entirely. The transaction can commit canonical UTXO state belonging to an orphan block. The same missing tip-continuity rule also allows arbitrary forward jumps if their parent happens to exist. ApplyBlock should accept only an exact replay of the current tip or the immediate next child of the tip.
A header-only or partial block can currently be marked fully indexed. The domain model explicitly allows Transactions to be nil while TxCount remains populated, because header-style RPC data may know the count without carrying bodies. But ApplyBlock loops over whatever transactions happen to be present and then advances sync_state; it never checks len(block.Transactions) == block.TxCount. A block claiming 200 transactions with zero supplied bodies could therefore become the durable checkpoint. The store boundary must reject incomplete blocks before inserting anything.
The claimed immutable-body protection is row-by-row, not whole-object complete. insertOrVerifyIdempotent only proves a specific natural-key row is identical if that row already exists. Inputs, witness items, and outputs are each independently inserted by index. Therefore the same txid could later be supplied with an extra input/output index that did not previously exist, and that new row would simply be inserted. Conversely, an incoming representation that silently omits an existing input/output/witness item is never detected. The same problem exists for witness stack completeness under one wtxid. This contradicts the stated invariant that “same txid + different body” and “same wtxid + different witness” must raise ErrImmutableConflict. We need whole-set cardinality/index verification after persistence, with rollback on mismatch.
Canonical mutations are not serialized, and markSpent ignores whether its guarded UPDATE actually changed a row. ApplyBlock reads sync_state without FOR UPDATE, while RollbackTo also performs its canonical-state rewrite without locking that row first. checkPrevout reads an unspent row, then markSpent later runs UPDATE ... WHERE spent=false, but does not inspect RowsAffected. With concurrent store users, two operations can reason from stale canonical state. A SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on sync_state('main') at the start of every Apply/Rollback gives us a simple global canonical-state mutex across processes, and markSpent should still verify the update result.

There is also one reorg capability gap tied to item 1: a previously orphaned block cannot become canonical again. RollbackTo deliberately leaves the block row with canonical=false. If that exact branch later wins again, applyBlockHeader verifies the immutable header but never restores canonical=true; when that block is the next valid tip, the final checkpoint trigger rejects it. A verified orphan block needs to be safely re-promotable when it is the immediate child of the current canonical tip.

