The first two findings are the ones I consider most urgent. The current code’s “lower-height blocks don't move the checkpoint” behavior sounds harmless, but because ApplyBlock also changes utxo_state and address caches, it creates a real path for orphan-derived canonical state to be committed without ever passing the checkpoint's canonical-block trigger.

Likewise, the chain.Block model deliberately distinguishes TxCount from the optional full transaction slice, so the store must explicitly refuse a partial block; otherwise a header-level object can become a “fully indexed” checkpoint.

Once these are fixed, the design should be considerably stronger before we connect it to live Core RPC.
